   113	## 2. Bundle format
   114	
   115	### 2.1 Members
   116	
   117	A V1 bundle carries these twelve members. Nothing is taken from outside the bundle except the trust configuration
   118	(section 5). The last column is the container member that carries each one (section 15.1). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G14,
   119	G17]
   120	
   121	| # | Member | Source | Container member |
   122	|---|---|---|---|
   123	| 1 | Unsigned profile label (read first, for dispatch) | [ENC §8:742-743] | top-level `profile` |
   124	| 2 | The receipt tuple `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)`, or `A_sig`, which encodes it | [ENC §5.3:493-497] | `a_sig` only (one tuple route) |
   125	| 3 | The manifest frame `F_manifest` and each key's possession signature over it | [ENC §4:362-369; A9:311-312] | `manifest`, `possession` |
   126	| 4 | The two public keys, checked against the manifest fingerprints | [ENC §5.3:509] | `keys` |
   127	| 5 | The DNSSEC snapshot (envelope per ENC §6) and its own OTS proof | [ENC §6:536-542; DD:94] | `dns` |
   128	| 6 | The git key-assertion evidence: raw signed commit object, the tree objects on the path, the blob, and the OTS proof stamped on the commit id | [ENC §7.2:706-710; A9:282-284] | `git` |
   129	| 7 | The receipt's OTS proof and the anchor transaction id | [SPEC R-3.42:679-682] | `receipt_anchor` |
   130	| 8 | Bitcoin block headers needed by the proof, each of which must be in the pinned set | [A9:150; DD:99] | `headers` (one pool) |
   131	| 9 | The verification specification bytes (content-addressed) | [A9:152-153; ENC §3.2:281] | `spec` |
   132	| 10 | The conformance vector bytes (content-addressed) | [A9:152-153; ENC §3.2:281] | `vectors` |
   133	| 11 | The plain-language purpose preamble, bound by hash in the signed bytes | [A9:153; ENC §3.2:281] | `preamble` |
   134	| 12 | The dependency manifest (section 9) | [A9:153-154] | `dependency_manifest` |
   135	| — | Observed anchor delay and issue-time policy version (R-3.46), optional diagnostics | [SPEC R-3.46:698-700] | `issuer_record` |
   136	
   137	- Members 9, 10 and 11 are bound by SHA-256 of their raw bytes, inside the signed common bytes. They are never signed
   138	  or anchored on their own. [ENC §0:71-72; ENC §3.2:281]
   139	- The OTS proof, txid, headers and confirmation evidence are outside `A_sig`. [ENC §5:402-405]
   140	- The txid sits in the bundle outside the signed bytes. [SPEC R-3.43:685-687]
   141	- **Container.** One canonical JCS JSON text, no JSON numbers, top level exactly
   142	  `{"layers":[L],"profile":"tessera-v1-strict"}`, with the closed layer schema of section 15.1. A required member
   143	  that is absent, at any nesting level, gives `UNVERIFIABLE / MEMBER_MISSING:<path>`; this rule precedes the generic
   144	  schema rule. A present nonconforming member or an unnamed member gives `INVALID / CONTAINER_SCHEMA`. The receipt
   145	  tuple is taken only by decoding `a_sig`. Re-indenting a container makes it `INVALID / JSON_NONCANONICAL`
   146	  (a visible custody restriction: keep the bytes, do not reformat). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G14, G17] *(Was:
   147	  "`OPEN (other):` the container and transport encoding, member names and member order", 2026-10-13.)*
   148	- **Observed delay and policy version** are carried in the optional `issuer_record` member: unsigned diagnostics.
   149	  Absence never affects the verdict; a present record is schema-checked (`INVALID / CONTAINER_SCHEMA`); its values
   150	  never alter verification parameters and are not authenticated observations. The honest issuer always emits it.
   151	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G33] *(Was: "`OPEN (other):` where the observed anchor delay and the issue-time policy version
   152	  are recorded", 2026-10-13.)*
   153	
   154	### 2.2 What the bundle never supplies
   155	
   156	The bundle never supplies the trust configuration, δ, ε, k or any verifier policy. [A9:148; SPEC R-5.9:1219]
   157	
   158	## 3. Verifier dispatch, stages and precedence
   159	
   160	### 3.1 Dispatch
   161	
   162	1. The verifier reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first. An unrecognized label gives
   163	   `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing else is evaluated. [ENC §8:742-743; A9:272]
   164	2. Out-of-profile layer structure or request gives the same result without evaluation. [DD:70; A9:272]
   165	   Cases c (two-layer transplant) and h (renewal wrapper) land here. [A9:173, 179]
   166	3. **Dispatch reads exactly three things**, in order, and nothing else [OWNER 2026-10-14, G17]:
   167	   - **D1.** The bytes are not strict JSON (ENC §1.4 checks 1–5), or exceed 2^27 bytes: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   168	     OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
   169	   - **D2.** Top-level `profile` absent or not exactly `tessera-v1-strict`: same.
   170	   - **D3.** `layers` not an array of length 1: same (cases c and h).
   171	   - **D4.** The request policy is a **verifier input**, never a bundle member; its only value is `strict`. Any other
   172	     request: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, detail `REQUEST_UNSUPPORTED`, with no evaluation (case d1's waiver
   173	     refused).
   174	   *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` what exactly dispatch inspects … Decide the fields dispatch reads, and where the request
   175	   type lives", 2026-10-13.)*
   176	4. Once dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every codec rejection is `INVALID`. That includes a signed `profile` other
   177	   than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version. [ENC §8:744-746]
   178	5. The one in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns the bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg`. [ENC §8:747-748;
   179	   SPEC R-2.37:308]
   180	
   181	### 3.2 Stages
   182	
   183	The stage names follow the spec's. The spec registers no order between stages, only that the verdict does not
   184	depend on evaluation order. [SPEC:1237-1248; SPEC R-5.16:1250] There are ten stages after dispatch. The
   185	table presents ENC/SPEC material; it is not a newly fixed execution order (section 0.1, item 6).
   186	
   187	| Stage | What V1 does | Source |
   188	|---|---|---|
   189	| (a) Parse | Decode every frame, the manifest and `A_sig` under ENC §1 to §5. Reason codes are reported in ENC's order. | [ENC §1.4:172-190; ENC §3.1:241-248; ENC §5:385-394] |
   190	| (b) Type | Tag must be in T and be the expected one: `TAG_UNKNOWN`, `TAG_MISMATCH`. No wrapper stage: one layer only. | [ENC §2:215-218; A9:155] |
   191	| (c) Algorithm | A well-formed unknown `common.alg` gives `UNVERIFIABLE`. A malformed, missing or substituted id gives `INVALID`. | [ENC §3.2:276; SPEC V13-V15:1282-1284] |
   192	| (d) Key binding | Each presented key hashes to its slot's manifest fingerprint (`KEY_MISMATCH`). `manifest_sha256` equals the hash of the manifest frame. | [ENC §5.3:509; ENC §3.2:280] |
   193	| (e) Authority | DNS channel and git channel (section 6), the issuance window, possession proofs. | [A9:137; A9:338-340] |
   194	| (f) Signature set | Both slots are required, from the signed manifest, never from what the bundle presents. Common bytes are byte-equal across slots. | [SPEC V28-V31:1327-1330; ENC §3.4:303-320] |
   195	| (g) Integrity | `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. Each committed `S` verifies over its committed `F`. Any difference from the committed tuple gives `RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`; a bad signature gives `SIG_INVALID`. | [ENC §5.3:498-511] |
   196	| (h) Temporal | The three-conjunct predicate at k = 6, plus the T rule (section 7). | [A9:138; SPEC V33-V36:1342-1346; SPEC R-5.19:1352] |
   197	| (i) Revocation | Not evaluated. The report says so. | [A9:248-252] |
   198	| (j) Partition | Section 3.3. | [SPEC R-5.25:1368] |
   199	
   200	- The issuance-window check is part of stage (e) (C4). [A9:338-340; ENC §7.1:636-643]
   201	- **Reported reason.** The reported reason is the first failed check in the **total check order** of section 15.7;
   202	  if none failed, the first unperformable check in that order. The order is a reporting order, not an execution
   203	  order. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G31] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` which reason code is reported when several different
   204	  checks fail", 2026-10-13.)*
   205	- **`DNS_*` record class.** A TXT RRset that is present is run through ENC §7's fifteen-step order; every failure is
   206	  `INVALID` with ENC's code, whether or not DNSSEC was performable. An absent TXT group is `UNVERIFIABLE /
   207	  DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`. ENC's `DNS_RRSET` zero case stays a codec vector, unreachable from a V1 container.
   208	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G24] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict class of the `DNS_*` record rejections", 2026-10-13.)*
   209	- **Size gates.** An empty snapshot body or a payload over its cap gives `LENGTH_CAP`, before the JSON categories.
   210	  The gates are exact and separate: container 2^27 bytes, payload per ENC §1, snapshot body 2^24. [OWNER 2026-10-14,
   211	  G30]
   212	
   213	### 3.3 Precedence and verdicts
   214	
   215	- Order of decision: any required check failed gives `INVALID`; else any required check unperformable gives
   216	  `UNVERIFIABLE`; else `VALID_STRICT`. There is no waiver set, so the degraded branch never applies. [SPEC
   217	  R-5.25:1368; A9:155]
   218	- `INVALID` dominates `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:176; SPEC R-5.26:1370; SPEC R-2.33:300] Case d3 depends on it.
   219	- The unperformable rule applies to required checks only. [SPEC R-2.32:298]
   220	- A verdict is never a bare boolean. `UNVERIFIABLE` is never promoted. [SPEC R-2.34:302; SPEC R-5.38:1394]
   221	- No fallback, negotiation or normalization during verification. [SPEC R-5.18:1266; ENC §1.2:156-158]
   222	- **Aggregation.** Physical evaluation order is unrestricted, but reporting uses the fixed total check order (section
   223	  15.7). Early termination is permitted only when every earlier-priority reportable check is settled and every
   224	  stable field, including T, is determined. A failure prevents only checks whose own necessary input cannot be
   225	  obtained; it does not suppress independent receipt-anchor evaluation needed for T. Derived checks are unperformable
   226	  when necessary inputs cannot be obtained, and never pass by default. An unknown `alg` routes the slot as opaque
   227	  (Lemma 7a): signature and possession semantics under it are unperformable; independent schema and hash checks still
   228	  run. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G26] *Dated correction 2026-10-14: the resolutions draft's "a verifier may stop early only
   229	  on a failed check" is withdrawn; it let two schedules report different stable fields.*
   230	
   231	### 3.4 Output
   232	
   233	Every report carries these six fields, and they are the stable set that later verifiers must reproduce: the verdict,
   234	its reason code, T, `revocation`, `historical_security` and standing. [A9:156; A9:262-268]
   235	
   236	- `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`. [A9:248-252]
   237	- `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply: an authenticated
   238	  safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared with T and A2.1's chain-time uncertainty. [A9:253-259]
   239	- Standing: `ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`, reported separately and never folded in. [A9:166-167; SPEC R-1.16:163]
   240	- No aggregate checkmark. [SPEC R-5.47:1419]
   241	- **Report encoding.** The stable-output object is the JCS object `{"historical_security":"NOT_EVALUATED",
   242	  "reason","revocation":"NOT_EVALUATED","standing":"ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE","T","verdict"}`; the success
   243	  reason is `OK`. T is the RFC 3339 string of nTime(h*) when the receipt proof parses, its file digest equals SHA256
   244	  of the presented `a_sig` bytes, no attestation in it is contradicted, and it has a qualifying attestation;
   245	  otherwise `null`. T is reported whatever the verdict. Diagnostics (trust-config version and hash, spec hash,
   246	  per-check list) go in a separate wrapper object, outside the stable six. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G32]
   247	- Relying-party text must say that a T reported with `INVALID` is not evidence of a valid service receipt.
   248	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G32]
   249	- Relying-party text must say that `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can come from presenting a later proof, and that the
   250	  holder should be asked for the issuer's original proof. [A9:344-345]
   251	- Relying-party text must say that the verifier cannot check public publication of the git commit. [A9:413-418]
   252	
   253	## 4. Issue order (issuer side, binding on the build)
   254	
   255	1. Fix the manifest and common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   256	2. Build both frames.
   257	3. Sign each frame.
   258	4. Verify both signatures with the exact bytes to be committed.
   259	5. Freeze the signature bytes. Any re-framing happens before this point.
   260	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp its SHA-256. One stamp per receipt.
   261	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   262	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   263	
   264	[ENC §5.4:515-525; A9:278-281] That is eight steps in both sources. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a
   265	fresh stamp. The frame-only anchor is withdrawn, with no fallback. [A9:277; ENC §5.1:407-414]
   266	
   267	Issuer duties that are not verifier rules: an attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped [A9:341-342]; the
   268	`gpg` signer refuses a frame whose `declared_issue_time` is more than ε from its clock [A9:327-330]; the issuer never
   269	re-authorizes a key [A9:260]; public publication of the commit stays an issuer duty [A9:413-418]; the issuer
   270	re-captures the DNSSEC snapshot before shipping if it fails the snapshot rule [DD:94].
   271	
   272	Further issuer duties [OWNER 2026-10-14]:
   273	- **Chain-order pre-commitment.** Do not issue in a window until the key-assertion commit's stamp has k = 6
   274	  confirmations and the receipt will anchor at a higher height (A9 C4). Before honest issuance the issuer obtains and
   275	  validates the git assertion's k = 6 proof. A receipt stamped after the issuer has observed h_git + 5 anchors above
   276	  it, so it passes the verifier's `h_git + 5 < h_receipt` rule (section 6.3). [G02; RB §5]
   277	- **Snapshot c3.** Check all three conjuncts on the snapshot's own proof before shipping; recapture or retry if they
   278	  fail. A snapshot stamped before the receipt need not confirm before it. [G06]
   279	- **DNSSEC support.** Validate the snapshot under this profile's algorithm set (section 6.1); do not ship a receipt
   280	  whose chain would be `DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED`. [G16]
   281	- **Snapshot body.** Convert to the section 15.3 body before stamping. [G19]
   282	- **Signature output.** Both OpenPGP adapters emit signatures that satisfy the rule-O policy of section 8 before
   283	  signing is final and before stamping. [G11]
   284	- **Canonical container.** Emit the container as canonical JCS (section 15.1). [G17]
   285	
   286	## 5. Trust configuration
   287	
   288	It ships with the verifier and is never read from the bundle. [A9:148; SPEC R-5.8:1217; SPEC R-2.17:262] It holds:
   289	
   290	| # | Item | Source |
   291	|---|---|---|
   292	| 1 | Root KSK-2017 | [A9:148-149; DD:66] |
   293	| 2 | Root KSK-2024 | [A9:148-149; DD:66] |
   294	| 3 | Repository entries: D = `wamason.com`, the repository key, an inclusive signing interval | OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13 (section 6.3); A9.5 repository entries; [A9:63-65] |
   295	| 4 | A versioned set of pinned mainnet header hashes, authenticated by the release allowlist | [A9:149; DD:66]; A9.5 trust-configuration authority |
   296	| 5 | ~~The parameters δ, ε, k~~ | moved to the frozen spec catalog (section 1) [OWNER 2026-10-14, G12] |
   297	
   298	*Dated correction 2026-10-14:* item 5 previously read "The parameters δ, ε, k [A9:217; SPEC R-5.8:1217]". A
   299	trust-configuration release can be extended, so verdict-deciding parameters now live in the frozen spec catalog,
   300	which cannot. Item 3 previously read "The pair (D, repository root key)"; A9.5's repository entries also carry a
   301	signing interval.
   302	
   303	- **Authority: independent release allowlisting** (A9.5 "Trust-configuration authority", OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14).
   304	  The trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials.
   305	  Each verifier release compiles in the SHA-256 of exactly one trust configuration; the config file itself carries no
   306	  signature. Releases are signed by the **release key**, which neither the DNS credential, the repository key nor the
   307	  issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key and its access boundary. Capture of any
   308	  authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release, authorize a configuration hash, or replace the
   309	  compiled trust root or policy catalog. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise of the release key is
   310	  distribution compromise, a stated boundary (A9 "The boundaries"). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G08]
   311	  - A config whose SHA-256 is not the compiled hash, or that fails the section 15.5 schema, makes the verifier refuse
   312	    to run with exit error `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`: no verdict.
   313	  - Bootstrap: the release public key is published with the verifier's documentation and relying-party text, and its
   314	    fingerprint is in the freeze record. A new release key is introduced only by a release signed by the current one.
   315	  - `version` starts at `"1"` and increases by 1; `previous_sha256` is 64 zeros for version 1, else the prior config's
   316	    hash. Every report carries the config version and hash as diagnostics; reproducing a verdict exactly (A9.4) uses
   317	    the release that compiled that hash.
   318	  - Required test: a captured repository signer (and, separately, a captured DNS credential and the issuing AWS
   319	    account) signs a config adding its own KSK, repository key and pins; the verifier refuses it.
   320	  - **OPEN:** release-key custody, holder and access boundary; the release artefact and release-signature format
   321	    (section 14).
   322	  - *Dated correction 2026-10-14:* the 2026-10-13 resolutions draft proposed a dedicated trust-config key giving the
   323	    config a detached rule-O signature, with append-only enforced in CI. Withdrawn: key inequality does not establish
   324	    independence, and CI cannot stop an attacker who bypasses it (`CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md` F04).
   325	
   326	- **Root KSKs, three periods each.** The trust configuration records each KSK's signing and reliance periods
   327	  separately from its publication and revocation dates. [A9:153; A9:302-303] The instrument spike found that
   328	  conflating signing with publication falsely rejected every snapshot from 2026-10-11. [SR:73-78]
   329	- **Root rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time if some trusted KSK whose **reliance
   330	  period** contains that time validates it. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance lifetime L; the reliance
   331	  interval is [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is an acceptance-policy value from cited RRSIG validity evidence,
   332	  labelled observed, not a proven global maximum. The extension admits authentic pre-retirement evidence anchored
   333	  later and does not prove signing before retirement: a thief of a retired KSK can manufacture eligible evidence
   334	  during it (A9.5; AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-13, kind CORRECT). *Dated correction 2026-10-13: the earlier wording
   335	  ("maximum validity … it made"; "usable for that long and no longer") overclaimed provenance and is withdrawn.* The rule is existential and
   336	  order-independent: a candidate that fails is informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible
   337	  key has been tried. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure.
   338	  [A9.5, A9:296-303, OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13 after `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F2]
   339	- **Rollover control (required vector).** A snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must
   340	  pass. [A9:307-308] Further required vectors (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F2 dispositions): a failing trusted
   341	  candidate listed before a validating one, in both trust-list orders, must pass; an honest old root signed before
