   603	### 7.2 Header pins
   604	
   605	- Every bundle header must be in the pinned set. Otherwise the verdict is `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`.
   606	  There is no retarget logic, because the pins cover every accepted header. [A9:150; DD:99]
   607	- **Conflict.** A supplied header at a pinned height with a different hash gives `INVALID / HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`:
   608	  it is contradictory evidence. A9.1's "a bundle header outside the pins" is read as a header **at a height the pins
   609	  do not cover**. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G23] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict for a header that … conflicts with a
   610	  pin", 2026-10-13.)*
   611	- **Coverage.** Required: headers h* … h*+5 of the selected attestation of each of the three proofs (receipt,
   612	  snapshot, git), all in the shared `headers` pool. Every supplied header, used or not, is checked against the pins.
   613	  For each pair of supplied headers at consecutive heights, a prev-hash mismatch gives `INVALID /
   614	  HEADER_CHAIN_BROKEN`; a missing intermediate height is not a broken link. No PoW, nBits or retarget check: a hash
   615	  equal to a pin implies all of it. At an unselected height, a pinned header with missing successors is ignored,
   616	  while an unpinned header blocks the bundle (`HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G07, G05] *(Was: "`OPEN
   617	  (step 8):` which heights the pins must cover for a given proof", 2026-10-13.)*
   618	
   619	### 7.3 The DNSSEC snapshot rule
   620	
   621	- The issuer anchors the DNSSEC snapshot with its own OTS proof. The envelope is ENC §6 under the tag
   622	  `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot`. [DD:94; ENC §6:536-542]
   623	- The verifier checks that the snapshot's anchored time lies inside every RRSIG window in the chain and within
   624	  [declared − ε, declared + δ]. The issuer re-captures before shipping if it does not. [DD:94] This is an owner
   625	  decision carried in, not routed. [A9:99-101]
   626	  - "declared" is the receipt's signed `declared_issue_time`, not the snapshot's capture time. Capture-to-anchor
   627	    latency is a separate observation, not this predicate. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F6, accepted 2026-10-13.)
   628	  - "every RRSIG window in the chain" means the one witness signature selected to authenticate each required RRset,
   629	    not every signature offered: an extra expired or invalid signature beside a valid one neither fails the RRset
   630	    nor narrows the joint window. The instrument's selection order is (key tag, inception, expiration, signature
   631	    bytes), first that validates. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F5, accepted 2026-10-13.)
   632	  - **Frozen witness rule.** The selected witness is the **first eligible validating** candidate in (key tag,
   633	    inception, expiration, signature bytes, canonical RRSIG RDATA, canonical key RDATA) order. For the root, reliance
   634	    eligibility precedes selection. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G35] *(Previously described only as the instrument's order.)*
   635	  - Conjunct 3 (`confirmed_at` of the snapshot ≤ declared + δ) is a new owner acceptance restriction, fail-closed. A
   636	    failure of any conjunct gives `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_TIME`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06]
   637	- The chain is evaluated at the snapshot's anchored time, not at T of the receipt. [A9:137]
   638	- Why: the joint RRSIG window measured 1.6 days, shorter than δ (M8). [DD:93]
   639	- **Snapshot body.** A fixed-order sequence of uncompressed RFC 1035 wire RRs in RFC 4034 §6.2 canonical form,
   640	  section 15.3. Framing violations are `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`; a syntactically complete body lacking a required
   641	  RRset or its witness RRSIG is `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING` (root: `NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`). The conversion
   642	  from resolver messages happens before stamping and changes S, so each snapshot needs a fresh stamp; earlier
   643	  stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the snapshot body
   644	  format", 2026-10-13.)*
   645	- **Snapshot time.** T_snap = T of the snapshot's own proof under the section 7.1 rule, at the same depth k = 6, and
   646	  `confirmed_at`(snapshot) likewise. DNSSEC is evaluated at T_snap only; no capture time, receipt T or wall clock is
   647	  ever used. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` how the snapshot's anchored time is derived",
   648	  2026-10-13.)*
   649	- **RRSIG time arithmetic.** Inception and expiration are 32-bit values v, each resolved to the unique absolute
   650	  second t with t ≡ v (mod 2^32) and |t − T_snap| < 2^31 (RFC 4034 §3.1.5, RFC 1982). If v − T_snap ≡ 2^31 (mod 2^32)
   651	  the candidate fails with `RRSIG_TIME_AMBIGUOUS`; if resolved inception > expiration it fails with
   652	  `RRSIG_INTERVAL_REVERSED`; inception = expiration is allowed. These are candidate failures under the existential
   653	  rule, ranked with `RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME`. The witness check is inception ≤ T_snap ≤ expiration, never "now",
   654	  including in 2106. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G27]
