    79	### Byte, hash and vector accounting (replaces "no signed byte changes")
    80	
    81	**No resolution changes the attestation-frame layout** (E§3–§5, `A_sig`, the manifest frame), and no existing
    82	fallback P8 golden or rejection vector needs rewriting merely to add above-codec policy. The following **do** get new
    83	hashes or signatures:
    84	
    85	1. **Spec and vectors objects.** Freezing edited V1-PROFILE/ENCODING-V1 text and the new conformance vectors changes
    86	   the `spec`, `vectors`, `preamble` and `dependency_manifest` hashes. Receipts issued under the frozen spec carry the
    87	   new `common.spec.sha256` and related hashes in their signed common bytes.
    88	2. **DNSSEC snapshot body.** Converting from resolver-message JSON to the G19 body changes S and `SHA256(S)`. Each
    89	   snapshot needs a fresh stamp. Previously stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused.
    90	3. **Trust configuration.** New bytes, a new hash compiled into a verifier release, and a new release signature by
    91	   the release key (G08).
    92	4. **Signature output.** G11's policy constrains what the issuer's OpenPGP adapters may emit before signing and
    93	   stamping.
    94	5. **Hybrid branch.** If taken, the authorized supplement changes the `kms` slot encoding and requires its own
    95	   golden and rejection vectors (A9.6). That is an existing A9 obligation, excluded from any "no change" statement.
    96	6. **Archived proofs.** Existing archived proof bytes cannot be converted in place while retaining their stamp.
    97	
    98	New **non-P8** conformance vectors are required for the container, OTS, snapshot body, git evidence, trust
    99	configuration, signature profile and full-profile verdicts (§L). A9.5 already requires the git cases before the
   100	freeze.
   101	
   102	**NARROW-MODEL:** none established. One conditional author surface remains: if release-key custody cannot be made
   103	independent of every authority-channel credential (G08), a channel-capture path must be excluded, which is a
   104	NARROW-MODEL for the author. See §K.
   105	
   106	---
   107	
   108	## Principles applied
   109	
   110	1. **Fail-closed.** Where two readings differ, the one that cannot return `VALID_STRICT` on evidence the other
   111	   rejects wins, unless it falsely rejects receipts from the documented issue path. Tensions are named per gap.
   112	2. **Pruning equivalence, bounded (time cluster).** An OTS proof is unsigned, outside `A_sig` (E§5), and can be
   113	   pruned to any one of its attestations without breaking the digest. Whoever presents a receipt can therefore already
   114	   choose which verified attestation sets T (A9 C5: "presentation-sensitive T"). Within the coverage contract A9 C6
   115	   now registers, ignoring a branch without headers yields no new cryptographic false accept. It does **not** make
   116	   contradictory evidence harmless: a contradicted branch fails the proof. Nor does it make two different selection
   117	   rules agree on the same input (G03).
   118	3. **Verdict class.** Present evidence whose check runs and fails gives `INVALID` (R-2.31). Absent evidence gives
   119	   `UNVERIFIABLE` (case d2). After dispatch, every codec rejection gives `INVALID` (E§8). Where a later, append-only
   120	   trust configuration could legitimately cover the evidence, the class is `UNVERIFIABLE` (A9.5: "can improve an
   121	   earlier UNVERIFIABLE result"). Where no later configuration could, it is `INVALID`.
   122	4. **Verdict stability (A9.4).** Policy that decides a verdict (algorithm, parameters, binding forms, D, DNSSEC
   123	   algorithm set) lives in the **frozen spec catalog** compiled into the verifier, not in the trust configuration.
   124	   This moves P §5 item 5 (δ, ε, k) out of the trust configuration. A9.4's contract is exact stable-field identity
   125	   for identical declared inputs. It is **not** a monotonic-verdict promise across configurations (G09).
   126	
   127	---
   128	
   129	## A. Container and evidence formats: G17, G14, G21, G19, G20
   130	
   131	### G17: container and dispatch — OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit)
   132	
   133	- **Resolution.** A bundle is one UTF-8 JSON text. It must pass E§1.4 checks 1–5 and 7: strict UTF-8, RFC 8259
   134	  syntax, depth, duplicate names, string rules, and canonical JCS. It contains **no JSON numbers**. Heights and
   135	  durations are decimal strings with grammar `0|[1-9][0-9]*` and value at most 4294967295 (no aliasing by leading
   136	  zeros). Binary values are RFC 4648 §4 base64 with padding that must round-trip (no non-zero pad bits, no line
   137	  breaks). The size cap is 2^27 bytes.
   138	
   139	  Top level is exactly `{"layers":[L],"profile":"tessera-v1-strict"}`. Dispatch, in order:
   140	  - **D1.** The bytes are not strict JSON (checks 1–5), or they exceed the cap: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
   141	    No label can be read.
   142	  - **D2.** `profile` is absent or not exactly `tessera-v1-strict`: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
   143	  - **D3.** `layers` is not an array of length 1: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`. This covers cases c and h.
   144	  - **D4.** The request policy is a **verifier input**, not a bundle member. Its only value is `strict`. Any other
   145	    request gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` (detail `REQUEST_UNSUPPORTED`) with no evaluation, which is case
   146	    d1's "waiver refused".
   147	
   148	  After dispatch, rules apply in this order (F11): (1) a non-canonical container gives `INVALID / JSON_NONCANONICAL`;
   149	  (2) **G14's absence rule**, including nested evidence members, and A9.5's `git.object_format` outcomes; (3) any other
   150	  schema failure gives `INVALID / CONTAINER_SCHEMA`.
   151	- **Layer `L` (closed schema).** Every value is base64 unless marked. Cardinality is exactly one of each.
   152	
   153	  | Member | JSON type and content | Invariants |
   154	  |---|---|---|
   155	  | `a_sig` | string: `A_sig` bytes (E§5) | decoded by E§5 |
   156	  | `manifest` | string: `F_manifest` | decoded by E§4 |
   157	  | `possession` | object `{"gpg","kms"}`: each a detached signature over `F_manifest` per its slot rule | both keys present |
   158	  | `keys` | object `{"gpg","kms"}` (G21) | both keys present |
   159	  | `receipt_anchor` | object `{"ots","txid"}`; `ots` base64; `txid` 64 lowercase hex, display order | |
   160	  | `headers` | object `{"<height>":"<160 lowercase hex>"}`: one shared pool for all three proofs | height grammar above; nonempty |
   161	  | `dns` | object `{"ots","snapshot_body"}` (G19), both base64 | |
   162	  | `git` | object `{"blob","commit","object_format","ots","trees"}`; `trees` an array of exactly 2 base64 strings [root, sub]; `object_format` a string | A9.5 descriptor rules |
   163	  | `spec`, `vectors`, `preamble`, `dependency_manifest` | string: raw bytes | hashed only (G13) |
   164	  | `issuer_record` | **optional** object `{"observed_anchor_delay_s","policy_version"}` (G33) | see G33 |
   165	- **Rationale.** A9.5 fixes dispatch on an unsigned label and `OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch. DD:70 names "profile,
   166	  layer structure or request", and an explicit `layers` array makes case c and case h dispatch failures, not schema
   167	  `INVALID`s. JSON reuses the strict parser the verifier already needs, so the container adds no codec. Banning
   168	  numbers keeps the container clear of `ES`. The container is unsigned transport, so it adds no P8 construction.
   169	- **Supported-path limit, visible.** Requiring canonical JCS makes a container that a holder's tool re-indented
   170	  `INVALID`, which is permanent. It gains no security, because every member is checked byte for byte. It is a new
   171	  custody restriction, not an inherited rule. The holder's custody text must say: keep the bytes, do not reformat.
   172	  (Codex noted the prototype issuer emits indented JSON; that is an instrument, not a conforming V1 issuer.)
   173	- **Rejected.** ZIP or tar: a new parser dependency. A tagged binary TLV: a new P8 construction. A request field
   174	  inside the bundle: then an attacker sets it.
