   190	### G21: transported public keys — CLARIFY (with branch exception)
   191	
   192	- **Resolution.**
   193	  - `keys.gpg` is the **bare v4 public-key packet body**, exactly the `gpg-sha256` preimage of E§3.3: no `0x99`
   194	    prefix, no packet header, no user IDs, no subkeys, no armour.
   195	  - **F10 text:** "gpg must parse as the specified v4 P-256 public-key body. kms must parse as P-256 DER SPKI under
   196	    the fallback, or as the exact ML-DSA-65 SPKI specified by the dated branch supplement under the hybrid. The rule
   197	    is selected by the installed governing spec and slot, never by key bytes alone." Otherwise `INVALID / KEY_FORMAT`.
   198	  - Each key must hash to its slot fingerprint (`INVALID / KEY_MISMATCH`).
   199	  - Under the fallback the kms slot's OpenPGP rule-O signature is verified with the SPKI point. Its OpenPGP wrapper
   200	    creation time is unbound (E§3.3, accepted).
   201	- **Rationale.** These are exactly the bytes the fingerprints commit to. No subkey heuristic. Hybrid signed encodings
   202	  and vectors remain an A9.6 obligation and are excluded from every "no change" statement.
   203	- **Rejected.** An OpenPGP certificate or transferable public key: subkey selection forks.
   204	
   205	### G19: DNSSEC snapshot body — OWNER CHOICE (body format) + NARROW-CLAIM (no wildcards)
   206	
   207	- **Resolution.** `snapshot_body` is a concatenation of resource records. Each is in RFC 1035 §3.2.1 wire format,
   208	  **uncompressed**, in RFC 4034 §6.2 canonical form: the owner name and **every domain-name field inside RDATA**
   209	  (including the RRSIG signer name) are lowercase and uncompressed. Each RR is self-delimiting, so the body needs no
   210	  extra framing. This is the chain shape of RFC 9102 §4. V1 adds a fixed order and a closed set:
   211	  1. **Groups, in this order.** First the root DNSKEY group. Then, for each zone z below the root down to the TXT's
   212	     signer zone, a DS group at z followed by a DNSKEY group at z. Last, the TXT group at `_tessera-v1.<D>`.
   213	     - **Group boundary.** A group is a maximal run of RRs with the same owner name, CLASS IN, and either TYPE t (the
   214	       RRset) or TYPE RRSIG with Type Covered t (its witnesses). Within a group the RRset comes first, in RFC 4034
   215	       §6.3 canonical RDATA order with no duplicates, followed by zero or more RRSIGs covering it, in (key tag,
   216	       inception, expiration, signature bytes) order.
   217	     - Each z must be a proper descendant of the previous zone. A DS or DNSKEY RRSIG's signer name must be the zone
   218	       the RFC requires (the parent for DS; z itself for DNSKEY; the root for the root group). Every TXT RRSIG's
   219	       signer name must be the last z.
   220	  2. **Fields.** CLASS is IN. The TTL is equal within an RRset (RFC 2181 §5.2) and is otherwise uninterpreted:
   221	     validation uses the RRSIG's Original TTL (RFC 4035 §5.3.2).
   222	  3. **Framing versus completeness (F08 text).** "A truncated RR encoding, forbidden RR type, duplicate RR,
   223	     inconsistent owner/type within a group, or misordered supplied group is INVALID/SNAPSHOT_FORMAT. A
   224	     syntactically complete sequence lacking a required RRset or its witness RRSIG is UNVERIFIABLE/DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING
   225	     (or the root-specific NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE). Missing groups do not alone constitute order violations. All
   226	     domain-name RDATA fields are encoded canonically under RFC 4034 §6.2." An empty body is `LENGTH_CAP` (G30).
   227	     Forbidden types: any type other than DNSKEY, DS, TXT and RRSIG (SOA, NS, NSEC, NSEC3, CNAME, DNAME, …), and any
   228	     RR at a name outside the chain. CNAME and DNAME are not followed.
   229	  4. **No wildcards.** A TXT RRSIG whose Labels field is less than the owner's label count (a wildcard expansion,
   230	     RFC 4035 §5.3.2) gives `INVALID / DNS_WILDCARD`. This is a visible NARROW-CLAIM: the issuer publishes an
   231	     explicit record.
   232	  5. **Envelope.** The stamped preimage is E§6: `header ‖ u32 len ‖ body`, with 1 ≤ len ≤ 2^24.
   233	- **Rationale.** E§6 and P §7.3 leave the body to the profile. RRSIG verification needs canonical RR wire form
   234	  anyway (RFC 4034 §6). The instrument (`spike/capture_dns.py`) stores whole resolver messages as base64 inside
   235	  JSON; converting them is mechanical and happens **before stamping**. The conversion changes S and `SHA256(S)`, so
   236	  each snapshot needs a fresh stamp; previously stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused. P8's envelope construction
   237	  and existing vectors do not change. No conforming explicit-owner V1 receipt is shown falsely rejected after fresh
   238	  capture and conversion; the archived spike output is not a conforming V1 bundle.
   239	- **Residual, stated.** Under DNSSEC an ancestor zone (root, `com`) can sign data for names below it. V1 inherits
   240	  this trust (C4 "DNSSEC authenticated"), and the n = 2 git channel is what guards against it.
   241	- **Rejected.** Raw resolver messages: compression and flag noise. JSON presentation format: re-parsing ambiguity.
   242	  RFC 9102 order-independence: fail-closed prefers one order.
   243	
   244	### G20: OTS and Bitcoin evidence encoding — OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit)
   245	
   246	- **Resolution, OTS grammar** (the OpenTimestamps `DetachedTimestampFile` serialization, major version 1, as
   247	  specified here; python-opentimestamps 0.4.5 is the reference reader, not normative):
   248	  - **Header.** Magic `00 4f70656e54696d657374616d7073 0000 50726f6f66 00 bf89e2e884e89294`, then varuint major
   249	    version = 1, then file-hash op `08` (SHA-256 only), then the 32-byte digest, then one Timestamp, then end of
   250	    input.
   251	  - **Timestamp.** `Timestamp := (ff Item)* Item`, where `Item := 00 Attestation | Op Timestamp`.
   252	  - **Ops.** `Op := 08` (sha256) `| f0 varbytes` (append) `| f1 varbytes` (prepend). An argument is 1–4096 bytes,
   253	    and every message and result is at most 4096 bytes.
   254	  - **Attestations.** `Attestation := tag(8) varbytes(payload)`, with payload at most 8192 bytes. A Bitcoin tag
   255	    `0588960d73d71901` has a payload of exactly one varuint height, consumed exactly. A pending tag
   256	    `83dfe30d2ef90c8e` has a payload of varbytes(URI ≤ 1000); its URI is ignored.
   257	  - **Varuint.** LEB128 (7-bit groups, little-endian, high bit set for continuation), **shortest form only**, value
   258	    at most 2^32 − 1, so at most 5 groups. A redundant form (for example a trailing `00` group after a continuation)
   259	    gives `INVALID / OTS_FORMAT`. *This diverges from python-opentimestamps 0.4.5, which accepts redundant groups;
   260	    the instrument must be updated. Real calendars write shortest form.*
   261	  - **Bounds.** Recursion depth at most 256; at most 65536 ops and 4096 attestations per proof; proof bytes at most
   262	    2^20. Duplicate identical branches are permitted and have no effect (selection is existential, G05).
   263	  - **Rejections.** Any other op (SHA-1, RIPEMD-160, Keccak, reverse, hexlify), any other attestation tag, or any
   264	    limit or grammar violation gives `INVALID / OTS_FORMAT`. The file digest must equal SHA256 of the stamped
   265	    object: `A_sig` for the receipt (`INVALID / RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`), the E§6 envelope for the snapshot
   266	    (`INVALID / SNAPSHOT_STAMP_MISMATCH`), and `<40-hex commit id>\n` for git (`INVALID / GIT_STAMP_MISMATCH`).
   267	  - **Merkle link.** A Bitcoin attestation's message must be 32 bytes and equal bytes 36–68 of the bundled 80-byte
   268	    header at that height (the merkle root, internal order).
   269	- **Resolution, txid (F09, the supported-proof subset).** `txid` stays (R-3.42, R-3.43, registered: "the anchor
   270	  transaction's id … identity/coherence handle"). On the path from the file digest to the **selected** attestation
   271	  (G03), let the first two consecutive sha256 ops be the transaction pair, with input P. Let m be the message
   272	  immediately before the maximal run of append/prepend ops that precedes the pair. The proof is in the supported
   273	  subset only if:
   274	  1. P parses exactly, consuming every byte, as a Bitcoin transaction in **non-witness serialization**: version (4),
   275	     compact-size input count ≥ 1, inputs (32 + 4 + compact-size script length + script + 4), compact-size output
   276	     count ≥ 1, outputs (8 + compact-size script length + script), locktime (4). A SegWit marker (`00 01` after the
   277	     version) is not allowed. Compact sizes are shortest form.
   278	  2. m is 32 bytes and some output script is exactly `6a 20 ‖ m` (an `OP_RETURN` push of the path commitment).
   279	  3. `hex(reverse(SHA256d(P)))` equals `txid`. The remaining path from SHA256d(P) to the merkle root is the
   280	     declared transaction-to-block path and is verified by the merkle link.
   281	  Otherwise `INVALID / ANCHOR_TXID_MISMATCH`. If several attestation paths reach the selected height, any one of
   282	  them may satisfy this.
   283	- **Evidence.** All 127 real proofs in the repository (`timestamps/**`, the spike proofs) carry 344 Bitcoin and 500
   284	  pending attestations, using **only** sha256, append and prepend. On every one of the 344 Bitcoin paths the first
   285	  sha256 pair takes a 125-byte input that parses exactly as a non-witness transaction with an output `6a 20 ‖ m`,
   286	  where m is the message before a run of two ops (prepend, append). Checked read-only on 2026-10-14 with the
   287	  prototype environment's python-opentimestamps. (The draft's 126 files / 496 pending was one file short.)
   288	- **Rationale.** Codex's counterexample (a proof that first double-hashes the digest into a commitment hash, then
   289	  commits that in a real transaction) is a valid timestamp whose first double-hash is not a transaction id. The
   290	  subset rule rejects it visibly rather than mislabelling the coherence handle. It is not a forged timestamp or a
   291	  C1/C2 attack. The closed op and tag set is a scope restriction, not an A9 protection theorem: SHA-1 commitments are
   292	  weaker, but rejecting strong unused operations protects nothing.
   293	- **Rejected.** Requiring canonical re-serialization (library-specific sort order). Dropping the txid (registered).
   294	  Trial-parsing any pair as a transaction (the instrument's `_is_tx`): a heuristic. Making the txid advisory: that
   295	  would need a registered correction.
